<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.2.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- objective reminder (#2691): put the operator's objective back in front of the running agent
         every 10 minutes. This template is the SCHEDULER; the pass itself is
         scripts/objective-reminder.sh, which owns the byte budget and the delivery confirmation.

         ⛔ WHY IT IS A JOB AND NOT A CONTROLLER TICK. The controller cycles, the lanes work, every
         health predicate is green — and the fleet can still be optimising the wrong thing. Drift is
         not a failure any process read can see; the only datum that catches it is the objective,
         repeated into the agent's own surface. So the reminder must run even when (especially when)
         nothing else looks wrong.

         ⛔ THE PAYLOAD HAS A BYTE BUDGET AND THE SCRIPT REFUSES OVER IT (measured: ~969 B →
         `Failed to write to socket (Broken pipe)`, ~520 B reliable; a ~750 B version silently stopped
         delivering for three consecutive ticks). The budget is enforced INSIDE the script (MAX_BYTES,
         rendered below) and not here, because the text is composed at run time; what this plist
         guarantees is that the script is invoked on a clock so a REFUSED pass is LOUD (its log line,
         and the inventory row) instead of invisible.

         ⛔ DELIVERY IS CONFIRMED BY A TURN BOUNDARY, NOT BY THE SEND'S EXIT STATUS:
         `latest_submitted_at` on the cmux workspace advances when the message becomes a TURN.
         `latest_submitted_message` LAGS and produced a false UNSENT on a channel that was working;
         the send's own exit 0 means bytes-on-a-socket, which is exactly the state a composer holding
         the text is in. The script judges the send in the NEXT pass (the boundary lags), and the one
         same-pass verdict it can prove is UNSENT (the payload is still in the composer).

         ⛔ TCC / WHY THE TARGET IS THE FARMED COPY. An earlier version of this template pointed at
         `{{AGENT_INFRA_PATH}}/scripts/objective-reminder.sh` and reasoned that `install-launchd.sh`
         "hard-fails a job whose ProgramArguments target does not resolve", so it must not point at a
         farmed copy that may not exist. MEASURED 2026-11-03: on this box `AGENT_INFRA_PATH` is
         `~/Documents/GitHub/agent-infra`, macOS TCC denies every launchd-spawned process access to
         `~/Documents`, and the job ran **44 times with `last exit code=126`**
         (`/bin/bash: …: Operation not permitted`) while `launchctl list` showed it loaded. The path
         resolved, so the guard passed it; nobody could tell "armed" from "working".
         The farmed `~/.pi/agent/scripts/` copy is the only target launchd can READ, and setup.sh's
         `fleet_srcs` array now delivers it. The objection above is answered rather than ignored: if
         the farm is missing, `verify_targets` refuses the install LOUDLY (exit 1, nothing installed)
         instead of arming a unit that cannot start — which is the whole point of the guard.
         ⛔ And `verify_targets` now also verdicts a TCC-protected EXEC target: that unit is SKIPPED
         loudly and nothing is installed for it (a different verdict from a MISSING target, which
         still fails the install), so this defect cannot be reintroduced as an armed-and-dead job by
         a future edit that "simplifies" the path back to `{{AGENT_INFRA_PATH}}`.

         ⛔ THE SURFACE IDS ARE WRITTEN OUT, NOT INHERITED. A launchd job gets neither the operator's
         shell env nor its rc files, so an unset WS/SURFACE would fall back to the script's defaults —
         and on a box with more than one workspace those defaults are whatever the script was born
         with. They are pinned here so `cat` of the installed plist answers "which surface is being
         reminded?" without reading code. Change them here, not in a shell profile.

         ⛔ EXIT 0 ALWAYS, AND THE CAPTURE IS ONLY FOR A DEATH BEFORE THE LOG EXISTS. The reminder's
         decision is its own bounded log ($HOME/.pi/agent/state/objective-reminder.log), which
         `scripts/lib/fleet-inventory.sh` reads (freshness + the last verdict token: a trailing
         `REFUSED`/`FAIL`/`UNSENT` is FAILING even though the exit code is 0 — that is the silent
         stop this pair exists to surface). The /tmp capture below exists for the one case the script
         cannot log: it did not start at all (a bash parse error, a missing interpreter) — the same
         class of failure that took the controller down, so it must not be silent here either.

         ⛔ ON A LINUX CELL there is no launchd and this template is not installed; the cell gets
         templates/systemd/eldato-objective-reminder.{service,timer} through install-cell-timers.sh,
         on the SAME systemd timer mechanism the cell already uses for its disk reaper. No second
         scheduler is invented (no cron: `crontab` writes fail with EINTR on this Mac — recorded). -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.eldato.objective-reminder</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/objective-reminder.sh</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <key>WS</key>
        <string>443A7DFB-3A05-4318-9606-451C768D1874</string>
        <key>SURFACE</key>
        <string>0D4F1EFD-C705-468F-8D00-85ACA3FD1AB4</string>
        <key>MAX_BYTES</key>
        <string>420</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <!-- 600s = 10 min: the cadence the live box was hand-armed at. Tighter would not help (the
         objective does not change per minute) and costs a cmux round trip plus a read-screen each
         time; looser lets a drifted objective stand for longer than one lane's lifetime. -->
    <integer>600</integer>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>
    <!-- ⛔ Deliberately NOT RunAtLoad: a login/boot pass would race the agent's own startup and its
         composer may not exist yet, which produces an UNSENT line for a box that is merely booting.
         The first reminder is one interval after the job is loaded, and `install-launchd.sh`
         bootstraps it immediately, so the wait is bounded by 10 minutes. -->
    <!-- ⛔ /tmp, like every other template here: launchd opens both files BEFORE exec, and nothing
         creates $HOME/.pi/agent/state for it. The durable record is the script's own log. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/objective-reminder.launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/objective-reminder.launchd.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
